+pages at the pane width, scrolled down, each page painted while it is within a
+screen of view and cleared once it leaves, so a long document holds a few
+canvases. The controls above the pages, a two-finger pinch and Ctrl/⌘ + wheel
+zoom by re-painting the pages at the new width, keeping the point under the
+pointer or between the fingers in place and letting the viewer scroll in both
+directions. `pdfZoom.ts` holds the scale steps, the stack geometry and the
+canvas budget. The percentage and actual-size target measure the first page at
+its physical size, 96 CSS pixels per inch; every page is drawn at the same
+width, so a document with differently sized pages reports the first page's
+scale. pdf.js everywhere would have replaced working viewers with a
+poorer one. It is the legacy build, which carries polyfills that WebViews a few
+versions behind need, and the module and its worker are bundled assets imported
+on first use, never fetched from a CDN, so offline use and the VS Code CSP hold
+and nothing loads until a PDF opens there. It reads the file in one GET,
+because a Range request needs a CORS header the server does not allow to the
+Android app. PDFs that need pdf.js's CMap, standard font or wasm data (CJK text
+without embedded fonts, JPEG 2000 images) may lose that text or image.
+
+### Why pdf.js pages repaint at a capped size and not scale a bitmap
+
+A zoomed page has to stay sharp, so the canvas is painted at the width it is
+drawn at, not stretched from the fit bitmap. A page at a deep zoom would need a
+canvas the WebView may refuse, so `pageRenderScale` stops at eight million
+device pixels or 8192 pixels on the long side, and CSS stretches what remains.
+Reading zoom stays inside the budget; only an extreme zoom shows soft text. The
+zoom is a multiple of fit width, so a rotated phone or a resized panel keeps
+the same zoom instead of a fixed pixel width.

